.TH KARICHECK 8 "2026-11-14" "KariDNS" "KariDNS Manual"
.\" Generated from docs/karicheck.md by man/md2man.pl (make man): edit the Markdown manual, not this page.
.SH NAME
karicheck \- Configuration and Zone File Validation Utility for KariDNS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RS 2
.nf
karicheck conf [config_path]
\&
karicheck zones [config_path]
\&
karicheck zone <domain> [config_path]
\&
karicheck zone <domain> <zone_file_path>
\&
karicheck zone <domain> \-
\&
karicheck db [view] [config_path]
\&
karicheck \-\-json conf|zones|zone|db ...
\&
karicheck \-v | \-\-version
\&
karicheck \-h | \-\-help
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBkaricheck\fR is a static validation and syntax inspection tool for
\fBkaridns(8)\fR configuration files and zone files (BIND-style RFC 1035 master
files and djbdns/tinydns \fBdata\fR files). It is the KariDNS counterpart of
BIND's \fBnamed\-checkconf\fR / \fBnamed\-checkzone\fR.
.PP
It validates configuration files and zone files for syntax errors, structural
constraints, record-level field ranges, DNSSEC algorithm recommendations (RFC
8624), CDS/CDNSKEY delete signals (RFC 8078), and RFC 8976 ZONEMD message
digests. The configuration file is parsed with the same parser as \fBkaridns\fR.
.PP
When \fBconfig_path\fR is omitted, \fB/usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns.conf\fR is
used (or \fB/usr/local/etc/karidns.conf\fR if only that file exists).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP 8
\fBconf [config_path]\fR
Validate the configuration file (see Configuration checks).
.TP 8
\fBzones [config_path]\fR
Validate the configuration file, then every \fBtype master\fR / \fBtype
primary\fR zone declared in it (the type name is case-insensitive), using each
zone's \fBfile\-format\fR. Secondary, forward and program zones are not checked
and are reported as skipped. Prints the number of checked and skipped zones and
the number of errors at the end (\fBChecked N zones (M skipped). Errors: E\fR).
.TP 8
\fBzone <domain> [config_path]\fR
Look up \fB<domain>\fR in the configuration file and validate its zone file with
the zone's settings (\fBfile\-format\fR, \fBcatalog\-zone\fR, tags). If several
views define the zone, each view's definition is checked; the exit status is 1
if any of them fails.
.TP 8
\fBzone <domain> <zone_file_path>\fR
Standalone mode: parse and validate \fB<zone_file_path>\fR as a BIND-format zone
for \fB<domain>\fR without a configuration file. The third argument is treated
as a configuration file if it contains \fB.conf\fR, and as a zone file
otherwise. A relative path is resolved against the current directory; the server
resolves relative zone paths (also with \fB../\fR) against the directory it was
started from.
.TP 8
\fBzone <domain> \-\fR
The zone text is read from standard input (BIND format, at most 64 MiB; a bigger
input is refused with \fBio.stdin.too\-large\fR, it is not cut). The messages
and the JSON document call the file \fB<stdin>\fR. \fB$INCLUDE\fR is refused
(\fBzone.include\-stdin\fR): there is no directory to include from. This is for
a caller that holds the zone in memory (a web interface that validates an edited
zone before it writes it to the database) and wants no temporary file.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-json\fR \fIcommand\fR ...
Write the result as one JSON document on standard output (see JSON OUTPUT)
instead of the text lines. \fB\-\-json\fR comes before the command. Standard
output then carries nothing but the document, and standard error nothing at all
(except when the document cannot be written). The exit status is the same as
without \fB\-\-json\fR. \fB\-h\fR and \fB\-v\fR are not commands with
\fB\-\-json\fR: they are usage errors.
.TP 8
\fBdb [view] [config_path]\fR
Read the PowerDNS tables of \fBoptions { database { } }\fR (read only, through
\fBkaridns\-dbloader \-\-check\fR) and check the schema, every zone (with the
checks of \fBzone\fR), the settings texts, the views, and the zones of the
configuration that hide a database zone. With \fBview\fR, only the zones that
view gets are reported. A single argument is a view unless it contains a \fB/\fR
or ends in \fB.conf\fR. See Database checks.
.TP 8
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version and exit.
.SH VALIDATION CHECKS
.SS Configuration checks
.IP \(bu 2
Syntax of the whole file, including \fBinclude\fR files, with the same rules as
\fBkaridns\fR (undefined TSIG keys in \fBtsig\-key\fR / \fBallow\-transfer\fR,
duplicate zones/views/keys/acls, invalid option values, ...).
.IP \(bu 2
Boolean values other than
\fByes\fR/\fBtrue\fR/\fB1\fR/\fBno\fR/\fBfalse\fR/\fB0\fR are errors.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBalso\-notify\fR (in \fBoptions\fR, \fBview\fR and \fBzone\fR) and the
top-level \fBremote\-servers\fR / \fBprimaries\fR / \fBmasters\fR lists are
checked with the BIND 9.20 grammar (see \fBkaridns(8)\fR, "\fBalso\-notify\fR
and \fBremote\-servers\fR"). A syntax error names the file, the line and the
statement (\fB[ERROR] karidns.conf:12: zone 'example.test.' also\-notify:
expected ';' after '192.0.2.1' but found '}'\fR). These are errors: a list name
that is not defined (or an address that is not an IP address), a list that
includes itself or nests deeper than 8 levels, a list defined twice, a \fBkey\fR
that no \fBkey { }\fR block defines, a \fBport\fR outside 1-65535 or on a list
name, more than 256 addresses after the lists are expanded, and \fBtls\fR (not
implemented). The same checks apply to \fBprimaries\fR / \fBmasters\fR of a zone
(a list name is resolved through the same lists, a per-primary \fBkey\fR must be
defined) and \fBnotify\fR takes \fByes\fR, \fBno\fR, \fBexplicit\fR,
\fBprimary\-only\fR or \fBmaster\-only\fR in \fBoptions\fR, \fBview\fR and
\fBzone\fR (anything else is an error naming the line).
.IP \(bu 2
An unknown statement inside \fBzone { }\fR or \fBview { }\fR is an error
(\fB[ERROR] zone 'example.test.': unknown option 'alow\-transfer' ...\fR):
\fBkaridns\fR used to skip such a statement without a word, so a typo silently
left a zone open or closed. Options that BIND 9 documents for a \fBzone\fR or
\fBview\fR statement but KariDNS does not implement (\fBallow\-query\fR,
\fBinline\-signing\fR, \fBdnssec\-policy\fR, \fBauto\-dnssec\fR,
\fBkey\-directory\fR, \fBmax\-journal\-size\fR, \fBzone\-statistics\fR,
\fBrecursion\fR, ...) give a warning (\fB[WARNING] ... is a BIND option that
KariDNS does not implement; it is ignored\fR) and are skipped. An unknown key
inside \fBcontrol\-channel { }\fR is an error too (\fB[ERROR] control\-channel:
unknown option 'port' (supported: socket, secret, algorithm, allow\-group,
keys)\fR): \fBkaridns\fR refuses to start with it, so \fBkaricheck conf\fR does
not call such a file valid. The same holds for \fBoptions { }\fR and \fBkey {
}\fR, with the file and line (\fB[ERROR] karidns.conf:12: options: unknown
option 'bind\-adress' ...\fR); BIND options of \fBoptions { }\fR that KariDNS
does not implement (\fBrecursion\fR, \fBforwarders\fR, ...) give a warning, and
a \fBkey\fR without \fBsecret\fR or \fBalgorithm\fR is an error. \fBlogging {
}\fR and \fBcontrols { }\fR are strict as well (an unknown statement or option,
a channel with no or two destinations, an unknown category, an undefined
channel, an invalid size or severity, a \fBunix\fR control, an unknown key or a
duplicate address in \fBcontrols\fR are errors with the file and line); what
KariDNS accepts but does not do (\fBbuffered yes\fR, a \fBsize\fR without
\fBversions\fR, an \fBinet\fR control, \fBstatistics\-channels\fR) gives a
warning. Unknown top-level blocks are still skipped without a message.
.IP \(bu 2
Address match lists (BIND 9.20 ARM "Address Match Lists"): \fBallow\-query\fR,
\fBallow\-query\-on\fR, \fBallow\-transfer\fR, \fBallow\-update\fR and
\fBallow\-notify\fR (in \fBoptions\fR, \fBview\fR and \fBzone\fR),
\fBmatch\-clients\fR, \fBecs\-trusted\-resolvers\fR and \fBacl\fR take an
address, a prefix, \fBany\fR, \fBnone\fR, \fBlocalhost\fR, \fBlocalnets\fR,
\fBkey <name>\fR (not in \fBecs\-trusted\-resolvers\fR; in \fBmatch\-clients\fR
it selects the view by the TSIG key of the request), a defined \fBacl\fR name,
or a nested \fB{ ... }\fR list, each optionally negated with \fB!\fR; in
\fBallow\-update\fR the bare name of a defined key is accepted as well. Anything
else is an error, and so is a statement written twice in the same block,
\fBport\fR / \fBtransport\fR in front of the braces of \fBallow\-transfer\fR, an
\fBacl\fR that contains itself, lists nested more than 8 deep, and
\fBtransfer\-acl\-match\fR / \fBupdate\-acl\-match\fR with an
\fBallow\-transfer\fR / \fBallow\-update\fR that has a negated key (the flat
evaluation they ask for cannot say that). Errors name the place of the element
(\fB[ERROR] karidns.conf:12: zone 'example.test.' allow\-query: entry 'internal'
is not an address, prefix, any, none, localhost, localnets or a defined acl\fR,
\fB... key 'k9' is not defined in any key {} block\fR, \fB... expected ';' after
the element but found '}'\fR). \fBlocalhost\fR and \fBlocalnets\fR are expanded
from the interfaces of the host \fBkaricheck\fR runs on where the flat lists
need them; the server evaluates them against its own start-up snapshot (see
karidns(8) "Address match lists"). A view whose \fBmatch\-clients\fR names a
\fBkey\fR and that follows a view which surely matches every client (\fBany\fR,
\fB0.0.0.0/0\fR with \fB::/0\fR, no \fBmatch\-clients\fR) gets a \fB[WARNING]\fR
with \fBfile:line\fR ("KariDNS warning (BIND does not check this) ... can never
be chosen"); it is not an error.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWarnings when a list means something else since the first-match evaluation\fR
(karidns(8) "Address match lists"): for a zone that writes its own
\fBallow\-transfer\fR with no \fBtransfer\-acl\-match\fR, a list that has
addresses and \fBkey\fR elements now lets in a client from a listed address
\fBor\fR signed with a listed key, where it used to need the address \fBand\fR
the key (more permissive: \fB[WARNING] Zone 'example.test.': allow\-transfer
lists addresses and keys: it now lets in ...\fR); write \fBtransfer\-acl\-match
all;\fR to keep the old meaning, or \fBany\fR to say it on purpose. For
\fBallow\-update\fR with keys and negated entries, the order now decides, so a
negated entry before a key refuses that key's requests (stricter;
\fBupdate\-acl\-match any;\fR keeps the old meaning). No warning is printed for
a list of addresses only, for a list with an explicit \fBtransfer\-acl\-match\fR
/ \fBupdate\-acl\-match\fR, or for a zone whose ACL comes from \fBoptions\fR or
\fBview\fR (those statements did nothing before).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBserver <prefix> { ... }\fR and \fBkey { }\fR in \fBview { }\fR (BIND 9.20;
karidns(8) "\fBserver\fR" and "\fBkey\fR"). Errors with file and line: an
unknown, ancient (\fBrequest\-sit\fR, \fBsupport\-ixfr\fR) or twice-written
option, a bad value, bits beyond the prefix length (\fB'192.0.2.1/24' is an
invalid prefix (extra bits specified)\fR), the same prefix twice in one list
(\fBalready exists, previous definition: file:line\fR),
\fBtransfer\-source\-v6\fR / \fBnotify\-source\-v6\fR for an IPv4 server and
\fBtransfer\-source\fR / \fBnotify\-source\fR for an IPv6 server (\fB... is not
legal for an IPv4 server\fR), a \fBkeys\fR name that is not defined for \fBevery
view that uses the statement\fR (\fBserver '192.0.2.1': unknown key 'k' (in
view 'v2')\fR: a view's own keys count only in that view), a view key with the
name of a global key or a second key of one name in a view, and a key used in a
zone of a view (\fBtsig\-key\fR, \fBallow\-transfer\fR, \fBallow\-update\fR, a
\fBprimaries\fR / \fBalso\-notify\fR \fBkey\fR) that is neither global nor in
that view. Warnings (\fBaccepted, no effect in KariDNS\fR): \fBbogus\fR,
\fBtcp\-keepalive\fR, \fBpadding\fR, \fBsend\-cookie\fR, \fBrequire\-cookie\fR,
\fBquery\-source\fR, \fBquery\-source\-v6\fR; \fBpadding\fR above 512 says
\fBlowered to 512\fR. \fB[NOTE]\fR for a view that has \fBserver\fR statements
of its own while the top level has some: the top-level ones do not apply in that
view. The source addresses of \fBserver\fR statements are in the \fB[INFO]
Source addresses\fR line.
.IP \(bu 2
A logging \fBcategory\fR may list several channels (up to 8; BIND writes to all
of them). A \fBsize\fR without \fBversions\fR on a log file gives a warning: the
file stops at that size (BIND behaviour) instead of being truncated as earlier
KariDNS versions did.
.IP \(bu 2
BIND option values (karidns(8) "BIND option values"): \fBtcp\-idle\-timeout\fR
and \fBtcp\-initial\-timeout\fR are in BIND's unit of 100 ms, a number written
in the old KariDNS milliseconds needs the suffix \fBms\fR, and a bare number
above 1200 is an error with file and line (\fBoptions tcp\-idle\-timeout: 10000
is more than the maximum of 1200 (120 seconds) ... write 10000ms\fR); a value
below BIND's minimum gives the BIND warning (\fBvalue is out of range: raising
to 25\fR). \fBversion\fR, \fBhostname\fR and \fBserver\-id\fR take a quoted
string, \fBnone\fR (and \fBhostname\fR for \fBserver\-id\fR); a \fBserver\-id\fR
that differs from \fBnsid\fR is an error. \fBminimal\-responses\fR takes
\fByes\fR, \fBno\fR, \fBno\-auth\fR or \fBno\-auth\-recursive\fR in
\fBoptions\fR and in \fBview\fR (the view's value is the one its clients
get). \fBmax\-records\fR, \fBmax\-records\-per\-type\fR,
\fBmax\-types\-per\-name\fR, \fBmax\-zone\-ttl\fR, \fBserial\-update\-method\fR,
\fBrollback\-window\fR (a number of seconds or a time with units; \fB0\fR keeps
no previous version for \fBkarictl rollback\fR; a bad value is an error with
file and line), \fBmasterfile\-format\fR (\fBraw\fR is an error) and the
\fBcheck\-*\fR options are read in \fBoptions\fR, \fBview\fR and
\fBzone\fR. Both names of a buffer setting with two values
(\fBudp\-recvbuf\-size\fR and \fBudp\-receive\-buffer\fR, \fBudp\-bufsize\fR and
\fBmax\-udp\-size\fR) are an error. \fBdnstap\fR is read in BIND's grammar too
(\fBdnstap { auth; }; dnstap\-output unix "...";\fR; \fBdnstap\-output file\fR,
mixing the two grammars and \fBdnstap\fR without \fBdnstap\-output\fR are
errors). \fBcatalog\-zones\fR marks the zones it names; a name that is no zone
gives a warning. \fBupdate\-policy\fR rules are checked (an unsupported
\fBdeny\fR, \fBlocal\fR, a count such as \fBA(3)\fR, and \fBallow\-update\fR in
the same zone are errors; an unsupported \fBgrant\fR is dropped with a
warning). What is accepted without effect gives a warning (\fBcheck\-sibling\fR,
\fBcheck\-spf\fR, \fBcheck\-svcb\fR, \fBedns\-udp\-size\fR,
\fBtcp\-keepalive\-timeout\fR, \fBfstrm\-set\-*\fR, \fBdefault\-primaries\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcontrol\-channel { keys { } }\fR (karidns(8) "Restricted control keys"): an
\fBallow\-group\fR that names no group (or a bad gid) is an error with file and
line (the name is looked up on the host karicheck runs on); an unknown key name,
a key listed twice, two keys with one secret, \fBallow\-commands\fR without
\fBread\-only yes\fR and an unknown or test-only command name are errors with
file and line. A key listed in \fBkeys { }\fR that is also used for TSIG
anywhere (\fBallow\-transfer\fR, \fBallow\-update\fR, \fBallow\-notify\fR,
\fBallow\-query\fR, \fBmatch\-clients\fR, a zone's \fBtsig\-key\fR,
\fBprimaries\fR / \fBalso\-notify\fR entries, \fBserver { keys }\fR,
\fBupdate\-policy\fR, an \fBacl { }\fR block, \fBacl\-ceiling\fR) is a warning
with the place of the use: whoever holds the key for DNS could also control the
server.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtype program\fR zones: \fBprogram\fR must be set and be an absolute path, and
\fBprogram\-args\fR may have at most 62 entries, and there may be at most 256
program zones (errors); a \fBprogram\fR that is not executable and a
\fBprogram\-user\fR that differs from \fBoptions { user }\fR give
warnings. \fBallow\-program\-zones yes;\fR is required when any program zone
exists.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBalias\-resolver { ... };\fR (in \fBoptions\fR, in a zone, and in \fBoptions {
database { } }\fR): at most 8 entries, each an IP address (a host name is an
error).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlisten\-on\fR / \fBlisten\-on\-v6\fR / \fBblackhole\fR / \fBbind\-address\fR
(BIND 9.20 ARM "Interfaces", "blackhole"; karidns(8) "Listening addresses"): the
lists are evaluated against the interface addresses of the machine
\fBkaricheck\fR runs on, and \fB[INFO] Listening (UDP and TCP) on: ...\fR shows
the addresses that result (IPv6 as \fB[addr]:port\fR). Errors with file and
line: \fBtls\fR, \fBhttp\fR or \fBproxy\fR (not supported), an invalid or
repeated \fBport\fR, an unknown acl name or a \fBkey\fR element in the list, a
\fBbind\-address\fR element that is not an IP address, one address with two
ports (\fBoptions listen\-on: address 192.0.2.1 is listened on at port 53
(karidns.conf:2) and at port 5353\fR), more than 64 addresses. Warnings: an
address in a \fBlisten\-on\fR list that no interface of this machine has (BIND
does not listen on it either), an IPv6 address in \fBlisten\-on\fR (or IPv4 in
\fBlisten\-on\-v6\fR), and statements that select no address at all
(\fBkaridns\fR refuses to start then). A configuration written for another host
therefore warns about its addresses when checked here.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtype forward\fR zones must have \fBforwarders\fR; a forwarder equal to one of
the addresses the server listens on (\fBlisten\-on\fR, \fBlisten\-on\-v6\fR,
\fBbind\-address\fR, with their ports) gives a self-loop warning. A note
(\fB[INFO]\fR) reminds that in BIND \fBtype forward\fR is a resolver-side zone
type, so a BIND configuration that relies on it is not equivalent.
.IP \(bu 2
BIND 9.20 compatibility of paths and syntax: \fBoptions { directory }\fR must
exist and be a directory (errors; a relative one gives a warning, BIND
recommends an absolute path), and the relative \fBfile\fR, \fBinclude\fR,
\fBpid\-file\fR and log paths are resolved against it as \fBkaridns\fR does. A
class after a zone or view name is accepted when it is \fBIN\fR and an error
with file and line otherwise (\fBzone 'x.test.': class 'CH' is not supported
(only IN)\fR). The zone types \fBhint\fR, \fBstub\fR, \fBstatic\-stub\fR,
\fBmirror\fR, \fBredirect\fR (and \fBdelegation\-only\fR) give a warning with
file and line (\fBthe zone is not loaded\fR) and the rest of their block is
skipped, so a stock BIND \fBnamed.conf\fR passes with warnings
only. \fBin\-view\fR is checked as BIND does (\fBlib/isccfg/check.c\fR): the
zone must exist in a view that is defined earlier -- as a \fBzone { }\fR of it,
or, when that view takes zones from the database or has a catalog zone, as a
name that is looked for in the database or the catalog at run time and so cannot
be checked here (D1a, in-view phase 2) (\fB'in\-view' zone 'x.test.' does not
exist in view 'v1', or view 'v1' is not yet defined\fR; a type that cannot be
shared in KariDNS gives its own message), only \fBin\-view\fR, \fBforward\fR and
\fBforwarders\fR may be written with it (\fBzone 'x.test.': 'in\-view' used with
incompatible zone options\fR; \fBallow\-query\fR, \fBallow\-query\-on\fR,
\fBallow\-transfer\fR, \fBallow\-update\fR and \fBallow\-notify\fR are accepted
with the KariDNS \fBin\-view\-settings view\fR), and \fBin\-view\-settings\fR
takes \fBowner\fR or \fBview\fR; every message has the file and
line. \fBkaricheck zones\fR and \fBkaricheck zone\fR check a shared zone once,
in the view that owns it. \fBmax\-records\-per\-type\fR or
\fBmax\-types\-per\-name\fR above BIND's default of 100 gives a warning (the
value is applied as written). A \fBport\fR that is not a number in 1-65535 is an
error with file and line.
.IP \(bu 2
Every CIDR in \fBecs\-tags\fR and \fBlocation\-tags\fR (in \fBoptions\fR and in
zones) must be valid (the server refuses to start with one that is not, too);
\fBecs\-tags\fR without \fBecs\-enable yes;\fR gives a warning.
.IP \(bu 2
The lexer reports a \fB/* comment\fR or a quoted string that is never closed,
and a token longer than 4096 bytes, as syntax errors; an unknown top-level
statement is a warning that names it and its line (\fBunknown statement 'zonee'
is skipped\fR); an address-list element with host bits below the prefix length
(\fB10.0.0.1/8\fR) is a warning with file and line.
.IP \(bu 2
The placeholder secret of \fBkaridns.conf.sample\fR is an error for the
\fBcontrol\-channel\fR secret and for a key that an ACL, an \fBupdate\-policy\fR
rule or \fBcontrol\-channel { keys }\fR uses
(\fBconf.tsig.secret\-placeholder\-used\fR), and a warning for a key nobody
uses. A database configuration whose CONFIG message for the loader would exceed
1 MiB is an error.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtransfer\-acl\-match\fR and \fBupdate\-acl\-match\fR take \fBany\fR or
\fBall\fR (anything else is an error). A warning says when an option has nothing
to choose between: without \fBallow\-transfer\fR / \fBallow\-update\fR (the
zone's, the view's or \fBoptions\fR' counts), or with an ACL that lists only
addresses or only keys (see karidns(8) "Address match lists" and "Settings of
database zones").
.IP \(bu 2
\fBoptions { database { ... }; }\fR (no database connection is opened): the
driver is built into \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR (\fB\-\-drivers\fR);
\fBloader\-user\fR / \fBloader\-group\fR exist; \fBloader\-path\fR is a regular
executable owned by root (or by the user who runs \fBkaricheck\fR, standing in
for the user who runs \fBkaridns\fR, when that is not root) and not writable by
group or others (a loader that fails these checks is an error, is not executed,
and the check is made on the open file that is then executed, as karidns does);
a \fBloader\-user\fR / \fBloader\-group\fR / \fBuser\fR / \fBgroup\fR /
\fBprogram\-user\fR that resolves to uid or gid 0, also under another name
(\fBroot\fR, \fBtoor\fR, \fBwheel\fR), is an error (karidns refuses it); and not
writable by group or others; a SQLite3 file exists, is a database in WAL mode
and is not readable by everyone; a PostgreSQL \fBconninfo\-file\fR has mode 0600
or 0400, belongs to the loader user and names a UNIX socket or
\fBsslmode=verify\-full\fR (warning otherwise); with PostgreSQL the
\fBserver\-id\fR (the name of this server's row in \fBkaridns_server_status\fR;
1 to 64 bytes, the parser enforces that) gives a warning when it has characters
other than ASCII letters, digits and \fB. \- _ :\fR, and so does a missing
\fBserver\-id\fR when the host name (the default) is empty or longer than 64
bytes (then no status row can be written); a \fBserver\-id\fR together with an
\fBalias\-resolver\fR (in \fBoptions { database { } }\fR or in \fBoptions\fR)
gives a warning that ALIAS answers can differ between the servers that share the
database, because each server's resolver and the moment of its lookup decide
them (milestone B11b; the SOA serials and the zone transfers are the same; a
resolver named only in a zone's profile text is not seen; LUA records are never
run, so they need no warning); \fBacl\-ceiling\fR entries are valid and its keys
are defined. \fBacl\-ceiling\-match\fR must be \fBany\fR or \fBall\fR; \fBall\fR
gives a warning when the ceiling lists only addresses or only keys (that kind
alone decides then). A database block \fBwithout \fBacl\-ceiling\fB\fR gives a
warning: the ACLs a database zone carries (transfers, dynamic updates) refuse
every client until a ceiling is set.
.SS Zone checks
.IP 1. 4
\fBZone File Directives\fR:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\fB$ORIGIN\fR, \fB$TTL\fR, \fB$INCLUDE\fR (up to 16 nesting levels and 32 files
per zone), \fB$GENERATE\fR (BIND syntax, any RR type, at most 100,000 records
per directive; see karidns(8) "ZONE FILE FORMAT")
.IP \(bu 2
\fB$INCLUDE\fR follows the rules of the server (karidns(8) "ZONE FILE FORMAT"):
a relative name is read from \fBoptions { directory }\fR when \fBkaricheck\fR
knows one (a zone checked through a configuration), else from the directory of
the zone file; an absolute path or one that leaves the base through \fB..\fR is
accepted only inside the directory of the zone file or \fBdirectory\fR, and
anything else is an error (\fB$INCLUDE '/etc/hosts' is outside the directories
opened at startup (zone file dir, directory): ...\fR), so the verdict is the
same as \fBkaridns\fR' (standalone mode used to accept any absolute path).
.IP \(bu 2
TTLs: a \fB$TTL\fR, record TTL or \fB$GENERATE\fR TTL that is not digits with
optional \fBs\fR/\fBm\fR/\fBh\fR/\fBd\fR/\fBw\fR units, or above 2147483647, is
an error at its position (it used to become 3600); so are malformed SOA refresh,
retry, expire and minimum fields and a SOA serial that is not a plain number
0-4294967295 (BIND 9.20 \fBfromtext_soa()\fR; the same in the \fBZ\fR line of a
tinydns file); a number after a unit (\fB1h30\fR) is not a TTL. A zone in which
some record has no TTL at all (no \fB$TTL\fR, no earlier TTL, no SOA to take the
minimum from) gives a warning that names the file and line of the first such
record (\fBzone.db:12: no TTL specified for 'www.example.test.' ...\fR, the path
of the \fB$INCLUDE\fR file when it is in one) and how many records there are:
BIND rejects it ("no TTL specified"), \fBkaridns\fR serves those records with
3600.
.IP \(bu 2
Classes: a zone has the class of its first record; a record of another class is
an error at its line (\fBclass 'CH' != zone class 'IN'\fR, as BIND
\fBlib/dns/master.c\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRESINFO\fR is a singleton type (RFC 9606 \[u00A7]3): two different RESINFO
records at one name are an error (\fBmultiple RRs of singleton type\fR, the
server does not load the zone).
.IP \(bu 2
TXT-like types (\fBTXT\fR, \fBSPF\fR, \fBAVC\fR, \fBNINFO\fR, \fBRESINFO\fR,
\fBWALLET\fR): a \fB<character\-string>\fR of more than 255 octets gives a
warning with file and line, \fBBIND rejects this\fR; \fBkaridns\fR serves it
split into 255-octet strings.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\e# <length> <hex>\fR RDATA may be written in any number of tokens (up to the
512 fields of a line) for every type.
.IP \(bu 2
KariDNS steering directives \fB$LOCATION\fR, \fB$LOCATION\-TAG\fR,
\fB$ECS\-SUBNET\fR, \fB$ECS\-SUBNET\-TAG\fR: records that reference an undefined
location or ECS tag are errors
.IP \(bu 2
tinydns \fBdata\fR files (through a configuration file with \fBfile\-format
tinydns;\fR): invalid location prefix lengths (\fB/n\fR above 32, error),
duplicate location codes, malformed IPv6 fields of \fB3\fR/\fB6\fR lines, and
SRV/NAPTR/SSHFP field ranges of generic lines
.RE
.IP 2. 4
\fBZone Integrity & Structural Invariants\fR:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
Exactly one SOA record at the zone apex that every client sees, and at least one
NS record at the apex. A tinydns zone (one \fBZ\fR line per location) and a zone
with \fB$LOCATION\fR / \fB$ECS\-SUBNET\fR tags may hold more SOA records when
each of the others has a location, a time stamp or a tag; every SOA record of
the zone must have the same serial (error, RFC 2181 \[u00A7]6.1), none may be
restricted (or accompanied by another SOA) in a zone that holds DNSSEC records
(RRSIG, DNSKEY, NSEC, NSEC3: error, the signature of the SOA RRset would not
hold for every client), and a SOA restricted to a tinydns location the zone does
not define, or whose countdown time stamp is over, is seen by nobody
(warning). Several SOA records that every client sees are an error as
before. See karidns(8) "Several SOA records".
.IP \(bu 2
CNAME exclusivity (CNAME must not co-exist with other record types at the same
owner name, except DNSSEC RRs), CNAME loops, CNAME chains (warning), NS/MX
targets that point to a CNAME (error, RFC 2181 \[u00A7]10.3) and SRV targets
that point to a CNAME (warning, RFC 2782 "the name MUST NOT be an alias"; BIND's
\fBcheck\-srv\-cname\fR default is also a warning)
.IP \(bu 2
Out-of-zone records (owner not at or below the zone name, e.g. address records
for name servers outside the zone) give a warning and are left out of all
further checks, because the server does not load them (RFC 1034 \[u00A7]4.2);
the exit status is not affected
.IP \(bu 2
In-bailiwick NS targets (apex and delegations) must have an A/AAAA record in the
zone (error); in-bailiwick MX and SRV targets without A/AAAA give a warning
only, because no RFC requires an address for them. Records occluded by a
delegation give warnings
.IP \(bu 2
Inconsistent TTLs within one RRset (warning); RRs with a location, time stamp or
tag are not harmonized with RRs that every client sees, and an unrestricted and
a restricted RR of one name and type with different TTLs are reported as such (a
client that sees both gets an RRset with differing TTLs; the TTLs are kept)
.IP \(bu 2
SOA MNAME pointing to a CNAME (warning)
.IP \(bu 2
Meta-types (e.g. \fBOPT\fR, \fBTSIG\fR, \fBAXFR\fR) must not appear in zone data
.IP \(bu 2
PowerDNS ALIAS and LUA records (TYPE65401 / TYPE65402 of CLASS IN, see
karidns(8) "POWERDNS ALIAS AND LUA RECORDS"): an ALIAS name that also owns an A
or AAAA RRset, an ALIAS in a zone with DNSKEY, RRSIG, NSEC, NSEC3 or NSEC3PARAM
records, and malformed ALIAS / LUA RDATA are errors (the server does not load
such a zone); an \fB[INFO]\fR line counts the ALIAS records (A and AAAA at those
names are NODATA unless an \fBalias\-resolver\fR is configured), and a warning
says that LUA records are kept for transfers and never answered
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBcheck\-*\fR options of the zone's configuration set the severity of some
of the findings above and add checks that report nothing without them
(karidns(8) "BIND option values"): \fBcheck\-integrity\fR (the NS glue and the
MX / SRV addresses; \fBno\fR skips them), \fBcheck\-mx\-cname\fR (an MX target
that is a CNAME: error without the option), \fBcheck\-srv\-cname\fR (warning
without the option), \fBcheck\-wildcard\fR (a \fB*\fR label that is not the
first), \fBcheck\-dup\-records\fR (the same RR twice), \fBcheck\-mx\fR (an MX
target that is an IP address) and \fBcheck\-names\fR (host names of A, AAAA and
MX owners and of NS, MX, SRV and SOA MNAME targets). The size limits
\fBmax\-records\fR, \fBmax\-records\-per\-type\fR, \fBmax\-types\-per\-name\fR
and \fBmax\-zone\-ttl\fR (primary zones) are errors here because the server does
not load such a zone. \fBkaricheck db\fR passes the options-level severities to
\fBkaridns\-dbloader \-\-check \-\-zone\-checks\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Every record must be serializable to wire format
.IP \(bu 2
A name with two different CNAME records, or two different DNAME records, is an
error (\fBzone.pdns\-types\fR; BIND: "multiple RRs of singleton type"); the same
RR twice, and records that tinydns locations or \fB$LOCATION\-TAG\fR /
\fB$ECS\-SUBNET\-TAG\fR tags keep apart, are not
.IP \(bu 2
Zone file syntax that BIND also refuses: a \fB$\fR word that is not a directive,
\fB$ORIGIN\fR without a name, a record line that starts with a blank before any
record ("no current owner name"), a \fB\eDDD\fR escape above 255, \fB\e000\fR in
a field that is not a TXT string, an entry of a \fB$LOCATION\-TAG\fR /
\fB$ECS\-SUBNET\-TAG\fR definition that is not an address or CIDR; in tinydns
data a TTL that is not decimal digits and more than 4096 \fB%\fR location lines
.IP \(bu 2
Catalog zones (\fBcatalog\-zone yes;\fR): \fBversion.<zone> TXT "2"\fR is
required, and group TXT records without a member PTR record give warnings
.RE
.IP 3. 4
\fBRecord Field Validation\fR:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
A/AAAA addresses; SRV, NAPTR, CAA (RFC 8659 tags and flags), SSHFP, HIP, WKS,
GPOS, X25, ISDN, EUI48/EUI64, CSYNC, DSYNC and SVCB/HTTPS target fields
.IP \(bu 2
WKS: the protocol is a number (0-255) or \fBTCP\fR / \fBUDP\fR (any case), as
the server reads it; a port is a number (0-65535) or a service name from the
server's built-in table (RFC 1035 \[u00A7]3.4.2; the IANA names of the common
ports, e.g. \fBsmtp\fR, \fBhttp\fR, \fBdomain\fR); any other port gives a
warning, and the record is reported as an error because the server cannot load
it
.IP \(bu 2
NSEC3PARAM at the apex: Flags other than 0 are an error (RFC 5155 \[u00A7]4.1.2:
such an NSEC3PARAM MUST be ignored, and the server ignores it); a hash algorithm
other than 1 (SHA-1), an iteration count other than 0 (RFC 9276 \[u00A7]3.1:
MUST be 0) and a non-empty salt (RFC 9276 \[u00A7]3.1: SHOULD NOT) give
warnings; more than 100 iterations are an error, and more than 150 (RFC 9276
\[u00A7]3.2) are refused with \fBzone.pdns\-types\fR because the server does not
load such a zone (a zone file, a transfer and a database or presigned zone are
all held to this limit; BIND 9.20 only warns above 50 and loads the zone). A
warning is also given when no NSEC3PARAM is usable, or when the one the server
uses has no NSEC3 RRs
.IP \(bu 2
NSEC3 records are checked once per chain (RRs with the same hash algorithm,
iterations and salt), not once per RR: hash algorithm, reserved flag bits,
opt-out (RFC 9276 \[u00A7]3.1), iterations and salt as above, and a warning for
a chain that matches no NSEC3PARAM with Flags 0 (the server does not use it)
.RE
.IP 4. 4
\fBDNSSEC Algorithm & Digest Verification (RFC 8624 / RFC 8078)\fR:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
Evaluates DNSSEC algorithms in DNSKEY and RRSIG records against RFC 8624 status
recommendations (e.g., flagging deprecated SHA-1 or MD5 algorithms)
.IP \(bu 2
Validates DS digest types (warning on deprecated digests) and, for the known
types, the length of the DS / CDS digest (SHA-1 20, SHA-256 32, GOST 32, SHA-384
48 octets: RFC 4034 \[u00A7]5.1.4, RFC 4509 \[u00A7]2, RFC 6605 \[u00A7]5; an
error, as BIND refuses it); SSHFP fingerprints are checked the same way (SHA-1
20, SHA-256 32 octets: RFC 4255 \[u00A7]3.1.2, RFC 6594 \[u00A7]5), in a zone
file and in a tinydns \fB_\fR line
.IP \(bu 2
The algorithm numbers of the IANA registry are known: 17 (SM2SM3), 23
(ECC-GOST12) and the private 252-254 draw no "unknown algorithm" warning; SSHFP
algorithm 6 (Ed448, RFC 8709) is assigned; CAA \fBissuemail\fR (RFC 9495) and
\fBissuevmc\fR are known tags
.IP \(bu 2
The address records that another delegation of the zone names as an NS target
(sibling glue, RFC 9471) are not reported as occluded; BIND does not report them
either
.IP \(bu 2
ML-DSA-44 (algorithm 18, draft-westerbaan-dnssec-mldsa): warns when a
DNSKEY/CDNSKEY public key is not 1312 octets or an RRSIG signature is not 2420
octets
.IP \(bu 2
Recognizes RFC 8078 CDS and CDNSKEY delete signals (Algorithm=0 / DigestType=0)
without false-positive warnings
.RE
.IP 5. 4
\fBRFC 8976 ZONEMD Message Digest Verification\fR:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
The ZONEMD serial must match the SOA serial, and ZONEMD should be at the zone
apex.
.IP \(bu 2
ZONEMD records with scheme 1 (SIMPLE) and hash algorithm 1 (SHA-384) or 2
(SHA-512) are verified by computing the zone digest of RFC 8976 \[u00A7]3.3: RRs
in DNSSEC canonical form and order (RFC 4034 \[u00A7]6.2 and \[u00A7]6.3, with
RFC 6840 \[u00A7]5.1: owner names and the domain names in the RDATA of the types
listed there in lower case), duplicates digested once, occluded data and
non-apex ZONEMD RRs included, the apex ZONEMD RRs and their RRSIG left
out. Out-of-zone records are not part of the zone and therefore not part of the
digest.
.IP \(bu 2
Other schemes and hash algorithms are reported as not verified
(\fB[INFO]\fR). Two ZONEMD RRs with the same scheme and hash algorithm, and a
digest whose length does not match the hash algorithm, are errors (RFC 8976
\[u00A7]4).
.IP \(bu 2
Every ZONEMD with a supported scheme and hash algorithm must match. This is
stricter than the RFC 8976 \[u00A7]4 verifier rule (one matching ZONEMD is
enough): karicheck checks the zone before it is published, and a recipient may
support only one of the hash algorithms.
.IP \(bu 2
The digest is verified even when the zone has other errors.
.RE
.PP
Each zone check prints \fB[RESULT] Zone '<zone>': <n> error(s), <n>
warning(s)\fR followed by \fB[OK]\fR or \fB[FAIL]\fR. The counts are the numbers
of \fB[ERROR]\fR and \fB[WARNING]\fR lines printed for that zone, including the
ZONEMD verification; \fB[INFO]\fR lines are not counted.
.SS Database checks (karicheck db)
\fBkaricheck db [view] [config_path]\fR reads the PowerDNS tables that
\fBoptions { database { } }\fR names, \fBread only\fR, and reports what the
server would refuse or what looks wrong, before the data goes live. It links no
database library: it runs \fBkaridns\-dbloader \-\-check\fR (see
\fBkaridns(8)\fR "Settings of database zones") once per database the views read,
and prints what the loader finds in the style of \fBkaricheck zone\fR. The
loader reads through the same driver calls and the same reader as the production
load, so a zone that \fB\-\-check\fR accepts is a zone the loader sends, and the
other way round. Nothing is written to the database (the connection is
\fBquery_only\fR / \fBREAD ONLY\fR, no status row is written, no \fBLISTEN\fR).
.PP
\fB[view]\fR limits the output to the zones that view gets: those of its own
database, or, for a view on the shared database, those
\fBkaridns_domain_views\fR maps to it (or all, when a zone has no row). A single
argument is a view unless it contains a \fB/\fR or ends in \fB.conf\fR, in which
case it is the configuration file. \fBkaricheck conf\fR still does the checks
that need no connection; \fBkaricheck db\fR assumes it was run. It needs a
loader that can be executed and has the driver, and it checks the loader file
the way \fBkaridns\fR does before it runs it (a regular file owned by root, or
by the user who runs \fBkaricheck\fR when that is not root, and not writable by
group or others; a violation is an error, as in \fBkaricheck conf\fR); if
\fBloader\-path\fR does not exist, it says \fBDB support is not installed\fR
(the package without \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR).
.PP
\fBRights.\fR The check should see the database the way the loader of
\fBkaridns\fR does:
.IP \(bu 2
run by \fBroot\fR, \fBkaricheck\fR drops to \fBloader\-user\fR /
\fBloader\-group\fR before it executes the loader (it refuses to run the loader
as root, so \fBloader\-user\fR must be set); so does \fBkaricheck conf\fR for
\fB\-\-drivers\fR: the loader is never run as root, nor with a loader that
failed the owner and mode checks; the child gets an empty environment, working
directory \fB/\fR and no descriptor above 2;
.IP \(bu 2
run by \fBanother user\fR, it uses that user's rights.
.PP
The first \fB[INFO]\fR line of each database names the rights used. When the
database file or the \fBconninfo\-file\fR cannot be used with them, the message
names the file and the missing permission (\fBis not readable by the user ...:
it needs read permission\fR, \fBhas no search (x) permission on the
directory ...\fR, SQLite3: \fBis not writable by the user ...\fR because even a
reader of a WAL database writes the \fB\-shm\fR file, and its directory or the
\fB\-wal\fR / \fB\-shm\fR files must be writable), and no zone is read.
.PP
Every database gets a line \fB[RESULT] Database ...: N zone(s) read, E error(s),
W warning(s)\fR followed by \fB[OK]\fR or \fB[FAIL]\fR, then one summary line. A
zone the loader would refuse is reported with its first failing row, as the
loader does (one bad row fails the zone): \fBzone 'example.test.' (domains.id
3): records.id 41: ...\fR. Everything that came from the database is escaped
before it is printed. The exit status is 1 when any error was found or the check
could not be run, else 0 (warnings do not change it). With \fB\-\-json\fR each
finding is a message of the document (code, place and zone), and the zones are
the entries of \fBzones\fR (\fB"format": "database"\fR; \fBfile\fR is null).
.PP
.TS
lbw(51) lb
lw(51) l.
\&Check	\&Level
_
\&\fBSchema\fR	\&
T{
a PowerDNS table or column the loader reads is missing (\fBdomains\fR,
\fBrecords\fR, \fBdomainmetadata\fR, \fBcryptokeys\fR; all missing columns are
named)
T}	\&error
T{
none of the \fBkaridns_*\fR tables exists (a plain PowerDNS database), or one is
missing or incomplete (what it costs is said: no change log, no view mapping, no
profiles, no tags)
T}	\&warning
\&\fBkaridns_schema_version\fR is newer than 3 / has no row	\&error / warning
T{
\fBauto\-reload yes\fR and \fBkaridns_changes\fR is missing, or change log
triggers are missing (named)
T}	\&error
T{
PostgreSQL: \fBkaridns_changes\fR has no \fBxid\fR column (schema version 1: no
change log)
T}	\&warning
T{
PostgreSQL: the role cannot \fBSELECT\fR a table the loader reads, or
\fBcryptokeys.domain_id\fR
T}	\&error
T{
PostgreSQL: the role can read \fBcryptokeys.content\fR (the private keys)
T}	\&warning
T{
PostgreSQL: the role cannot \fBINSERT\fR / \fBUPDATE\fR
\fBkaridns_server_status\fR; only one of the multi-server tables exists;
\fBserver\-id\fR is set but they are missing
T}	\&warning
\&the schema named by \fBdatabase\-schema\fR does not exist	\&error
\&\fBEvery zone\fR	\&
T{
the reader's refusals, with \fBrecords.id\fR: a content the zone parser rejects,
an unsupported type, an owner outside the zone, a name that is not a domain
name, a prio that contradicts the content (MX / SRV), a TTL out of range, a row
that is not safe as a line of text, no enabled apex SOA, a \fBSLAVE\fR /
\fBCONSUMER\fR without usable \fBmasters\fR
T}	\&error
T{
\fBALIAS\fR in a \fBPRESIGNED\fR zone, \fBENABLE\-LUA\-RECORDS=1\fR, malformed
\fBALIAS\fR / \fBLUA\fR data
T}	\&error
T{
\fBLUA\fR records (kept for transfers, never answered); \fBALIAS\fR records
(\fB[INFO]\fR, NODATA unless an \fBalias\-resolver\fR is set)
T}	\&warning / info
T{
records with a NULL \fBttl\fR (\fBdefault\-ttl\fR is used); a \fBprio\fR on a
type other than MX / SRV; metadata that is ignored
T}	\&warning
\&the SOA serial is 0	\&warning
T{
every check of \fBkaricheck zone\fR on the zone (the very same code): missing NS
at the apex, CNAME with other data, glue and delegation rules, inconsistent TTLs
in an RRset, field ranges of SRV / NAPTR / CAA / SSHFP / HIP / WKS ..., DNSSEC
algorithm and digest recommendations, NSEC3 and NSEC3PARAM rules, ZONEMD
verification
T}	\&as in \fBkaricheck zone\fR
T{
a \fBPRODUCER\fR zone: the catalog zone itself is not served yet (decision D9, a
later 0.6.x release), but its member zones are (\fBits N member zones are
served\fR); secondaries that take the catalog from KariDNS will not get
it. Migrating from PowerDNS works; only the catalog zone is missing. Exit status
0 when this is the only finding
T}	\&warning
T{
\fBdomains.catalog\fR names a zone that is not a \fBPRODUCER\fR zone of the
database
T}	\&warning
T{
two \fBdomains\fR rows of one source whose names are equal ignoring case
(\fBExample.test\fR and \fBexample.test\fR; both drivers; the message names both
\fBdomains.id\fR): not supported, PowerDNS stores names in lower case, and a
full read and a one-zone read (reload request, auto reload) may pick different
rows
T}	\&warning
\&\fBKeys and presigned zones\fR	\&
T{
\fBcryptokeys\fR rows without \fBPRESIGNED=1\fR (PowerDNS would sign online,
KariDNS serves signed data only)
T}	\&error
T{
\fBNSEC3PARAM\fR metadata with a bad syntax; a presigned NSEC3 or NSEC chain
that does not match the zone (a name without its \fBRRSIG(NSEC3)\fR owner, an
\fBordername\fR that is not the hash, parameters outside RFC 5155 \[u00A7]10.3)
T}	\&error
\&NSEC3 iterations other than 0 (RFC 9276)	\&warning
T{
a presigned NSEC3 zone without an \fBRRSIG(NSEC3PARAM)\fR at the apex: the TTL
of the rebuilt \fBNSEC3PARAM\fR is the SOA MINIMUM, because the TTL the signer
used is unknown
T}	\&warning
\&\fBSettings (metadata and profiles)\fR	\&
T{
the settings text of a zone or of its profile (\fBkaridns_profiles\fR) does not
parse in restricted mode, the profile is not defined
T}	\&error
T{
the settings name a TSIG key that \fBkaridns.conf\fR does not define
T}	\&error
T{
an \fBallow\-transfer\fR / \fBallow\-update\fR / \fBecs\-trusted\-resolvers\fR
entry that lies outside \fBacl\-ceiling\fR, a key that \fBacl\-ceiling\fR does
not list, or ACLs while no \fBacl\-ceiling\fR is configured (a database ACL
refuses every client then)
T}	\&warning
T{
\fBloader\-state\-file\fR (D1a step 7): the directory does not exist or cannot
be written by the loader user (the loader writes \fB<file>.tmp\fR there and
renames it), the file cannot be read by it
T}	\&error
T{
\fBloader\-state\-file\fR that is not a valid hot list (empty, truncated, a bad
count or checksum: the loader ignores it and the next list replaces it); a
directory that everybody can write in; \fBloader\-state\-file\fR or
\fBwarm\-before\-serve\fR without \fBmemory\-limit\fR (\fBkaricheck conf\fR)
T}	\&warning
\&\fBZones of \fBkaridns.conf\fB and views\fR	\&
T{
a zone of the same name in the view of the configuration hides the database
zone: with \fBfile\fR / \fBtype master\fR (D-PRIO), \fBtype program\fR / \fBtype
forward\fR (D-PROG)
T}	\&warning
T{
a \fBkaridns_domain_views\fR row names a view that is not defined, or one that
does not read the database through the shared source
T}	\&warning
\&a zone that only such rows map is served by no view	\&warning
.TE
.PP
Examples (the paths are those of a test installation; \fBexample.test\fR and
friends are test names):
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
$ karicheck db /usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns.conf
[INFO] Loading config /usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns.conf...
[INFO] Checking Database file '/usr/local/etc/karidns/zones.sqlite3' (the default view) with the rights of the user dnsadmin (uid 1001)
[INFO] Database file '/usr/local/etc/karidns/zones.sqlite3': karidns_* schema version 2
[WARNING] Database file '/usr/local/etc/karidns/zones.sqlite3': zone 'example.test.' (domains.id 1): records.id 4: 1 record(s) have a NULL ttl; default\-ttl 3600 used (first shown)
[WARNING] Database file '/usr/local/etc/karidns/zones.sqlite3': zone 'example.test.' (domains.id 1): allow\-transfer entry '198.51.100.0/24' is outside acl\-ceiling; a client there is refused
[ERROR] Database file '/usr/local/etc/karidns/zones.sqlite3': zone 'broken.test.' (domains.id 2): records.id 9: invalid IPv4 address literal in A record
[WARNING] Database file '/usr/local/etc/karidns/zones.sqlite3': zone 'shadow.test.' (domains.id 3): D\-PRIO: the default view has a zone with this name in the configuration (file '/usr/local/etc/karidns/shadow.zone'); it hides the database zone
[RESULT] Database file '/usr/local/etc/karidns/zones.sqlite3': 3 zone(s) read, 1 error(s), 3 warning(s)
[FAIL] Database file '/usr/local/etc/karidns/zones.sqlite3' has errors.
[INFO] Checked 1 database (3 zones). Errors: 1, warnings: 3
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run by root, the same check says \fBwith the rights of
loader\-user 'karidns\-db' (karicheck was run by root and drops to it, as
karidns does for the loader)\fR. A package without the loader, and a database
file the user cannot read:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
$ karicheck db /usr/local/etc/karidns/noloader.conf
[ERROR] DB support is not installed: the database loader '/usr/local/etc/karidns/no\-such\-loader' does not exist (this installation has no karidns\-dbloader; install the karidns\-sqlite3, karidns\-postgresql or karidns\-db package, or set loader\-path)
\&
$ karicheck db /usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns.conf
[ERROR] Database file '/usr/local/etc/karidns/zones.sqlite3': database file '/usr/local/etc/karidns/zones.sqlite3' is not readable by the user dnsadmin (uid 1001): it needs read permission (mode 0000, owner uid 1002, group gid 0)
.fi
.RE
.SH JSON OUTPUT
\fBkaricheck \-\-json <command> ...\fR runs the same checks as the text mode and
writes one JSON document (RFC 8259, UTF-8) on standard output when the run is
over. A caller that always parses standard output gets an answer for every run,
a usage error included. Nothing else is written to standard output, and nothing
to standard error unless the document itself cannot be written (then the status
is 1 and a line says so).
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
{
  "karicheck": 1,
  "version": "0.6.2",
  "command": "zone",
  "result": "fail",
  "counts": { "error": 1, "warning": 2, "checked": 1, "skipped": 0, "dropped": 0 },
  "truncated": false,
  "messages": [
    { "severity": "error", "code": "zone.syntax", "text": "Syntax error in db/example.test.zone at line 12\enReason: invalid TTL", "file": "db/example.test.zone", "line": 12, "zone": "example.test.", "view": null }
  ],
  "zones": [
    { "zone": "example.test.", "view": null, "file": "db/example.test.zone", "format": "bind", "result": "fail", "errors": 1, "warnings": 2 }
  ]
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.TS
lb lbw(41)
l lw(41).
\&Field	\&Meaning
_
\&\fBkaricheck\fR	T{
The schema version, an integer. It is raised only when a field changes its
meaning or goes away; a new field does not raise it. Read the fields you know
and ignore the others.
T}
\&\fBversion\fR	\&The version of \fBkaricheck\fR.
\&\fBcommand\fR	T{
\fBconf\fR, \fBzones\fR, \fBzone\fR or \fBdb\fR (\fBimport\fR or \fBexport\fR
for \fBkaridns\-zone\fR); \fBnull\fR when the command line named none that
exists.
T}
\&\fBresult\fR	T{
\fBok\fR (exit status 0), \fBfail\fR (1: problems found) or \fBusage\fR (2:
usage error).
T}
\&\fBcounts.error\fR, \fBcounts.warning\fR	T{
The messages of those severities, also the ones that did not fit into
\fBmessages\fR.
T}
\&\fBcounts.checked\fR, \fBcounts.skipped\fR	T{
Zones checked and zones skipped (a type that has no zone file); for \fBdb\fR,
\fBchecked\fR is the number of zones read. 0 for \fBconf\fR.
T}
\&\fBcounts.dropped\fR, \fBtruncated\fR	T{
At most 10000 messages and 100000 \fBzones\fR entries are kept; the rest is only
counted. \fBdropped\fR is the number of messages left out, \fBtruncated\fR is
true when anything was left out.
T}
\&\fBmessages[]\fR	T{
Every finding in the order \fBkaricheck\fR found it.
T}
\&\fBmessages[].severity\fR	\&\fBerror\fR, \fBwarning\fR, \fBnote\fR or \fBinfo\fR.
\&\fBmessages[].code\fR	T{
The code of the problem (below); a string, in every message.
T}
\&\fBmessages[].text\fR	T{
The text the text mode prints, without the \fB[ERROR]\fR tag and the final line
feed. A continuation (\fBReason: ...\fR of a syntax error, the expected and
computed digest of a ZONEMD mismatch) follows after a line feed. May hold any
characters (it quotes names from the files).
T}
\&\fBmessages[].file\fR, \fB.line\fR	T{
Where the problem is: the file (\fB<stdin>\fR for standard input), and the line
(a positive integer) or \fBnull\fR when it is not known. For \fBdb\fR both are
\fBnull\fR.
T}
\&\fBmessages[].zone\fR, \fB.view\fR	T{
The zone (absolute name, as \fBkaricheck\fR shows it) and the view the message
is about, or \fBnull\fR. The view of a configuration without \fBview { }\fR is
\fB__default__\fR; \fBnull\fR when there is no view (a standalone zone file, a
message about the configuration as a whole).
T}
\&\fBzones[]\fR	T{
One entry per zone checked, in order: \fBzone\fR, \fBview\fR and \fBfile\fR
(\fBnull\fR when unknown), \fBformat\fR (\fBbind\fR, \fBtinydns\fR or
\fBdatabase\fR), \fBresult\fR (\fBok\fR or \fBfail\fR) and the numbers of
\fBerrors\fR and \fBwarnings\fR found while it was checked.
T}
.TE
.PP
\fBkaridns\-zone \-\-json\fR (\fBkaridns\-zone(8)\fR) writes the same document:
the first key is \fB"karidns\-zone": 1\fR instead of \fB"karicheck": 1\fR,
\fBcommand\fR is \fBimport\fR or \fBexport\fR, \fBzones[]\fR has one entry per
zone imported or exported (\fBfile\fR is the input or output file), and the
codes are the \fBdb.import.*\fR, \fBdb.export.*\fR and \fBdb.zone.*\fR ones
below (with \fBusage.*\fR, \fBio.*\fR, \fBconf.*\fR and \fBzone.*\fR).
.PP
All strings are valid JSON strings: \fB"\fR and \fB\e\fR are escaped, every
control character (below U+0020, and U+007F) is written as \fB\eu00XX\fR, and a
byte that is not part of valid UTF-8 (a zone file, a file name or the database
can hold anything) is replaced by U+FFFD. A text longer than 4096 bytes is cut
and ends with \fB...\fR.
.PP
\fBStability.\fR A code is never renamed and never used for another problem once
released. A code that is no longer printed stays in the list, marked retired. A
code names the problem, not the text: the same code may come with several texts
(they carry the details), and the text of a message may change without its
code. New codes may appear in any release; treat a code you do not know by its
\fBseverity\fR. The codes of \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR findings that carry none of
their own are \fBdb.finding\fR.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karicheck \-\-json zone example.test /etc/karidns/zones/example.test.zone
karicheck \-\-json zone example.test \- < edited.zone
karicheck \-\-json conf /usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns.conf
karicheck \-\-json db /usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns.conf
.fi
.RE
.SS Message codes
Severity is the usual one of the code; a check whose severity the configuration
sets (\fBcheck\-*\fR options) prints the severity in force.
.PP
